<section class="shell band">
  <div class="split">
    <div>
      <h2>What a horse notices</h2>
      <p>Prey animals survive by reading intention and arousal faster and more accurately than
      we do. A horse will move away from someone who is saying all the right things while
      holding their breath, and settle next to someone who has genuinely arrived. It is not
      mystical. It is a very good sensor pointed at exactly the thing therapy usually has to
      infer from words.</p>
      <p>That makes horses an unusually direct teacher of presence, and an unusually honest
      mirror for the gap between what you intend and what you are actually doing. People who
      have spent years being competent at therapy often meet something here they have been able
      to talk their way around indoors.</p>
      <p>The horse is <strong>Blu</strong>. He is not a prop and he is not trained to perform
      anything therapeutic; he simply responds to whoever is standing in front of him, which is
      the entire point.</p>
      <h3>What happens in a session</h3>
      <ul class="plain">
        <li>Arriving, and noticing what your body does on the way in.</li>
        <li>Being near the horse, without a task, longer than feels comfortable.</li>
        <li>Simple ground work: approaching, leading, stopping, asking.</li>
        <li>Talking about what just happened, which is where the therapy lives.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Why online sessions come first</h3>
      <p>One to three online sessions before the first equine session is not a formality. It
      establishes pacing, gives you a way to settle that you have already practiced, and means
      neither of us is meeting the other for the first time in a field. The same requirement
      applies to <a href="/therapy/walk-and-talk/">trail walk sessions</a>.</p>
      <h3>Who it suits</h3>
      <p>People who talk more easily when something else is happening. People whose
      self-awareness is strong in words and thin in the body. People working on boundaries,
      because asking a thousand-pound animal to move is boundaries with the abstraction
      removed.</p>
      <h3>Who it does not</h3>
      <p>Anyone under 18, anyone with a horse allergy, and anyone currently in crisis, for whom
      stabilization online comes first. If you are frightened of horses, that is worth
      discussing rather than ruling yourself out: fear is workable material, but it should be
      chosen rather than endured.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

